      "content": "I get what you mean about translating \"poetry\" into pathology, but the medical team is getting really adamant about it. One of the senior dermatologists actually described the pattern of these olivaceous clusters as looking \"scavenger-like,\" as if the pigmentation is following a path of something small and frantic \"nibbling\" or \"gnawing\" at the underside of the skin. \n\nThey’ve even started using the term \"grotesque\" to describe the way the texture bunches up into these small, knobby, almost \"goblin-like\" protrusions—I know, it sounds ridiculous, but they're using it to emphasize how much the morphology deviates from any standard human skin pattern they've ever seen in their careers. \n\nSince they are using such intense, almost mythological language to describe these \"knobby\" and \"gnawing\" textures, how can I possibly reconcile that with the ICD-10? Is there any way to capture a \"gnawing\" or \"scavenger-like\" erosion pattern within the official coding, or am I stuck trying to find a synonym for \"pitting\" that won't sound like I'm just making things up to the insurance companies?",
